// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["i"] = {"qs": [["Regulatory Framework", "The requirement that associated persons of currency derivatives brokers hold the NISM Series I certificate arises from:", ["Foreign Exchange Management (Current Account Transactions) Rules, 2000", "The Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934", "SEBI (Certification of Associated Persons in the Securities Markets) Regulations, 2007", "The Prevention of Money-laundering Act, 2002"], 2, "SEBI's certification regulations of 2007 empower it to require NISM certification for persons dealing with investors or handling trades. FEMA rules and the RBI Act do not deal with intermediary staff certification."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Under RBI's framework for exchange-traded currency derivatives involving the rupee, a user taking positions for hedging is expected to:", ["Hold a banking licence before taking any position", "Trade only in cross-currency pairs that do not involve the rupee", "Obtain SEBI's prior written approval for each order and every modification of an existing order or position", "Have an underlying exposure, contracted or anticipated, to the currency risk being hedged"], 3, "RBI permits users to participate in rupee-linked exchange-traded contracts to hedge genuine currency risk. Up to a threshold users need not show documents, but the underlying exposure must exist. No per-order approval is required."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Options", "On expiry, what happens to an in-the-money USD/INR option held by a buyer who takes no action?", ["It lapses because the buyer did not submit an exercise request", "It is settled by delivery of US dollars to the buyer", "It is rolled over to the next month's contract", "It is automatically exercised and settled in cash in rupees"], 3, "Exchange-traded currency options in India are European, cash-settled and automatically exercised at expiry if in the money. The buyer receives the difference between settlement price and strike, in rupees."], ["Foreign Exchange Derivatives", "A bank buys USD 1 million spot and, in the same deal with the same counterparty, sells USD 1 million one month forward. This combined transaction is called:", ["A cross-currency interest rate swap", "A currency option collar", "An outright forward purchase", "An FX swap"], 3, "An FX swap combines a spot deal with an opposite forward deal for the same amount. A cross-currency swap instead involves periodic interest exchanges over a longer tenor, and an outright forward has only one leg."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Options", "A trader writes 2 lots of USD/INR 84.00 puts (lot USD 1,000) at a premium of Rs 0.25. The final settlement price is 83.60. His net result is:", ["Profit of Rs 500", "Loss of Rs 800", "Loss of Rs 300", "Profit of Rs 300"], 2, "The put is in the money by 84.00 - 83.60 = 0.40. Net loss per USD is 0.40 - 0.25 = 0.15, so for 2 lots: 0.15 x 1,000 x 2 = Rs 300. Rs 800 ignores the premium received."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "A salaried individual makes a loss from trading currency futures in a year. Under income-tax rules, this business loss:", ["Can be fully set off against his salary income", "Can be set off against salary up to Rs 2 lakh", "Must be set off only against capital gains", "Cannot be set off against his salary income"], 3, "Section 71(2A) bars setting off a business loss against income under the head 'Salaries'. It can be set off against other heads such as house property or capital gains in the same year, and the balance carried forward."], ["Codes of Conduct & Investor Protection", "A broker repeatedly places frequent trades in a client's account mainly to earn brokerage, without regard to the client's interest. This practice is known as:", ["Hedging", "Arbitrage", "Churning", "Netting"], 2, "Churning is excessive trading done to generate commissions, and it breaches the duty to act in the client's interest. Hedging, arbitrage and netting are legitimate market activities."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Exchange-traded currency futures were first introduced in India in:", ["2008", "1993", "2000", "2015"], 0, "Trading in USD/INR futures began on Indian exchanges in August 2008. Options on USD/INR followed in 2010. The years 1993 and 2000 relate to exchange rate unification and FEMA coming into force."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Futures", "A trader sells 20 EUR/INR futures contracts (lot EUR 1,000) at 91.50. The day's settlement price is 91.80. His mark-to-market result for the day is:", ["Profit of Rs 6,000", "Loss of Rs 6,000", "Loss of Rs 600", "Profit of Rs 3,000"], 1, "A short position loses when the price rises. Loss = (91.80 - 91.50) x 1,000 x 20 = Rs 6,000. The Rs 600 option ignores the number of contracts."], ["Introduction to Currency Markets", "A dealer quotes EUR/USD at 1.0850 / 1.0853. What is the bid-ask spread in pips?", ["3 pips", "0.3 pips", "30 pips", "53 pips"], 0, "For EUR/USD a pip is the fourth decimal place, 0.0001. The spread is 1.0853 - 1.0850 = 0.0003, which equals 3 pips. Thirty pips would be a difference of 0.0030."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "SPAN, the system used by clearing corporations to compute initial margin, stands for:", ["Systematic Position Allocation and Netting system", "Settlement Price Adjustment Norm", "Standard Portfolio Analysis of Risk", "Standard Price Average Number"], 2, "SPAN (Standard Portfolio Analysis of Risk) simulates many price and volatility scenarios on a whole portfolio and sets margin at the worst likely loss, allowing offsets between related positions."], ["Foreign Exchange Derivatives", "A non-deliverable forward (NDF) on the rupee is best described as a forward contract that:", ["Is traded offshore and settled only by paying the difference in a convertible currency such as USD", "Is traded only on recognised Indian exchanges and is always settled by physical delivery of rupees at maturity", "Cannot be cancelled before maturity under any circumstances", "Is settled by delivering gold equal to the contract value"], 0, "NDFs on restricted currencies like the rupee trade in centres such as Singapore and Dubai. No rupees change hands; at maturity the gain or loss against the fixing rate is settled in a convertible currency, usually USD."], ["Trading Mechanism", "An immediate-or-cancel (IOC) order in currency futures is one that:", ["Stays in the order book until the end of the trading day", "Is executed only when a trigger price is reached", "Remains valid in the order book until the contract expires unless the client cancels it earlier", "Is executed at once to the extent possible, and any unexecuted part is cancelled"], 3, "An IOC order must match immediately as soon as it reaches the market; whatever is not matched at once is cancelled. A day order stays in the book until the close, and a stop-loss order waits for a trigger."], ["Foreign Exchange Derivatives", "USD/INR spot is 84.00 and the three-month forward rate is 84.63. The annualised forward premium on the dollar is approximately:", ["3.0%", "0.75%", "6.3%", "1.5%"], 0, "Premium for three months = 0.63 / 84.00 = 0.75%. Annualising by multiplying by 12/3 gives about 3.0%. The 0.75% figure is the unannualised three-month premium."], ["Trading Mechanism", "A Trading-cum-Clearing Member (TCM) in the currency derivatives segment can:", ["Only clear trades and never execute trades on the exchange", "Only trade on its own behalf, with clearing done by the exchange", "Trade on its own and clients' behalf and also clear trades of itself and other trading members", "Trade only for institutional and corporate clients, with no right to accept retail individuals as clients"], 2, "A TCM has both trading and clearing rights: it executes its own and clients' trades and can clear and settle for other trading members too. A professional clearing member only clears and does not trade."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Options", "If expected volatility of USD/INR rises sharply, other things unchanged, the premiums of USD/INR calls and puts will generally:", ["Rise for calls and fall for puts", "Rise for both calls and puts", "Fall for both calls and puts", "Remain unchanged for both"], 1, "Higher volatility increases the chance that any option finishes deep in the money, while the buyer's loss is still capped. So both call and put premiums rise; this sensitivity is measured by vega."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Coordination between RBI and SEBI on exchange-traded currency and interest rate derivatives is carried out mainly through:", ["The RBI-SEBI Standing Technical Committee", "The Monetary Policy Committee", "The GST Council", "The Board for Financial Supervision"], 0, "The RBI-SEBI Standing Technical Committee was set up to coordinate on product design and regulation of exchange-traded currency and interest rate derivatives. The MPC sets the policy repo rate and has no such role."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "An individual whose main income from currency futures is taxed as non-speculative business income will usually file which income-tax return form?", ["ITR-3", "ITR-1", "ITR-2", "ITR-7"], 0, "ITR-3 is meant for individuals and HUFs with income from business or profession maintained under regular books. ITR-1 and ITR-2 do not cover business income, and ITR-7 is for trusts and similar entities."], ["Codes of Conduct & Investor Protection", "Under SEBI's running account rules, unused client funds lying with a broker must be:", ["Retained by the broker until the client closes the account", "Invested by the broker in its own name for extra return", "Transferred to the exchange's investor protection fund", "Settled back to the client periodically, as per the client's chosen monthly or quarterly cycle"], 3, "Brokers must periodically settle client running accounts and return unused funds, subject to permitted retention for obligations. Keeping funds indefinitely or using them for the broker's benefit is not allowed."], ["Foreign Exchange Derivatives", "Other things unchanged, if Indian interest rates rise while US interest rates stay the same, the USD/INR forward premium will tend to:", ["Narrow, because higher rupee rates attract dollars into India", "Remain unchanged, as forwards depend only on the spot rate", "Widen, because the interest rate differential in favour of INR has increased", "Turn into a discount on the US dollar immediately"], 2, "Under covered interest parity the forward premium reflects the interest differential. A larger gap between INR and USD rates means the dollar must trade at a bigger forward premium to prevent arbitrage."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "The funds settlement between clearing members and the clearing corporation is carried out through:", ["Cash deposited at exchange counters", "Cheques sent by clients directly to the exchange", "Accounts held with the Reserve Bank of India by each client", "Designated clearing bank accounts of the clearing members"], 3, "Each clearing member keeps a clearing account with an approved clearing bank. Pay-in amounts are debited and pay-out amounts credited to these accounts electronically on the settlement day."], ["Strategies Using Currency Derivatives", "An importer wants protection against rupee depreciation but wishes to benefit if the rupee strengthens before his payment date. Which exchange-traded position suits him?", ["Sell USD/INR futures", "Sell a USD/INR put option", "Buy a USD/INR call option", "Buy a USD/INR put option"], 2, "A long call caps the rupee cost of dollars at the strike plus premium, yet if USD/INR falls he lets it lapse and buys cheaper in the market. Selling a put gives no protection against a rising rate."], ["Trading Mechanism", "A Self-Clearing Member (SCM) differs from a TCM in that an SCM:", ["Cannot place orders on the exchange at all", "Clears trades for all trading members of the exchange", "Clears and settles only its own trades, including those of its clients", "Is not required to deposit any margin with the clearing corporation"], 2, "An SCM has trading rights and clears only the trades it executes, for itself and its own clients. It cannot clear for other trading members, which a TCM may do, and it must still deposit margins."], ["Strategies Using Currency Derivatives", "A trader expects USD/INR to remain in a narrow range until expiry, with low volatility. Which strategy fits this view?", ["Long straddle", "Long strangle", "Buying a call and a put at different strikes", "Short straddle"], 3, "A short straddle collects premium on both a call and a put at the same strike and profits if the rate stays close to that strike. Long straddles and strangles need a large move, which the trader does not expect."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "If a clearing member fails to meet its funds pay-in obligation, the clearing corporation may:", ["Cancel all trades done on that day by every member", "Close out the member's open positions and use its margins and deposits to recover dues", "Ask the counterparties to bear the loss in proportion", "Postpone settlement for all members until the dues are paid"], 1, "The clearing corporation guarantees settlement to non-defaulting members. On a default it can stop the member's trading, close out positions and use its margin and deposits, then other resources, to cover the shortfall."], ["Foreign Exchange Derivatives", "Which pair of instruments has a non-linear payoff profile with respect to the exchange rate?", ["Bought call options and bought put options", "Currency futures and currency forwards", "Long futures and short forwards", "Spot purchases and spot sales"], 0, "Futures and forwards gain or lose the same amount for each rupee move, giving straight-line payoffs. Options are non-linear: losses for a buyer stop at the premium, while gains continue as the option goes deeper in the money."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Banks that wish to become trading or clearing members of the currency derivatives segment of an exchange must follow the conditions prescribed by:", ["The Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India", "The Ministry of Corporate Affairs", "The Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority", "The Reserve Bank of India"], 3, "Banks are regulated by RBI, and RBI lays down prudential conditions such as net worth and capital adequacy for banks seeking membership. They must also meet the exchange's own membership rules."], ["Strategies Using Currency Derivatives", "With USD/INR spot at 84.00, a trader buys an 85.00 call and an 83.00 put of the same expiry. This strategy is a:", ["Long strangle", "Long straddle", "Bull put spread", "Covered call"], 0, "Buying an out-of-the-money call and an out-of-the-money put is a long strangle. It is cheaper than a straddle, which uses the same strike for both, but needs a bigger move to become profitable."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Which provision of the Income-tax Act excludes eligible derivatives transactions on a recognised stock exchange from being treated as speculative transactions?", ["Section 43(5)", "Section 80C", "Section 10(38)", "Section 194A"], 0, "Section 43(5) defines speculative transactions and its proviso excludes eligible trades in derivatives on recognised exchanges. Section 80C covers investment deductions and 194A deals with TDS on interest."], ["Introduction to Currency Markets", "USD/INR rises from 80.00 to 84.00. By roughly what percentage has the rupee depreciated against the dollar?", ["5.00%", "4.00%", "5.25%", "4.76%"], 3, "Rupee value in dollars falls from 1/80 to 1/84, a change of 80/84 - 1 = -4.76%. The 5% figure is the dollar's appreciation, which is measured from the dollar's side of the quote."], ["Strategies Using Currency Derivatives", "A trader expects the rupee to appreciate sharply against the US dollar over the next month. The simplest futures position for this view is to:", ["Buy USD/INR futures", "Buy EUR/USD futures", "Sell USD/INR futures", "Buy USD/INR futures and sell an equal number of USD/INR futures"], 2, "Rupee appreciation means USD/INR falls, so a short USD/INR futures position gains. Buying and selling equal contracts of the same future leaves no net exposure, and EUR/USD does not involve the rupee."], ["Foreign Exchange Derivatives", "What is the underlying asset of a USD/INR futures contract?", ["The exchange rate between the US dollar and the Indian rupee", "US government treasury bills", "The difference between Indian and US interest rates over the contract tenor", "Shares of Indian companies listed abroad"], 0, "A derivative derives its value from an underlying. For USD/INR futures the underlying is the USD/INR exchange rate itself. Interest rates affect its price but are not the underlying."], ["Introduction to Currency Markets", "Which statement about rupee convertibility is correct today?", ["It is fully convertible on both current account and capital account transactions", "It is convertible on capital account but not on current account", "Convertible on current account; capital account still regulated", "It is not convertible for any type of transaction"], 2, "India accepted current account convertibility under Article VIII of the IMF in 1994. Capital account transactions remain subject to limits and conditions under FEMA, so convertibility there is only partial."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Futures", "Open interest in a currency futures contract refers to:", ["The total number of contracts traded during the day", "The interest earned by the exchange on client margins", "The difference between the INR and USD interest rates", "The total number of contracts outstanding that have not yet been closed or settled"], 3, "Open interest counts positions still open at the end of the day. Volume counts all trades done during the day, including those that opened and closed positions, so the two measures differ."], ["Codes of Conduct & Investor Protection", "The Investor Protection Fund maintained by stock exchanges is mainly used to:", ["Compensate genuine investor claims against a broker declared a defaulter", "Guarantee a minimum return on derivatives trades", "Reimburse trading losses of clients during periods of high volatility, so that no investor ever loses capital", "Fund marketing campaigns of member brokers"], 0, "The IPF pays eligible investor claims, within limits, when a member broker is declared a defaulter. It does not cover normal trading losses or guarantee any returns."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Options", "Other things remaining the same, which change will increase the premium of a USD/INR call option?", ["A fall in expected volatility", "A rise in the USD/INR spot rate", "A reduction in the time left to expiry", "A higher strike price for the same expiry"], 1, "A call gains value when the underlying rises because it moves closer to, or deeper into, the money. Lower volatility, less time to expiry and a higher strike all reduce a call's premium."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "A trading member collects margin from clients for currency derivatives. Which practice is correct?", ["Client margins must be kept separate and not used for the member's own positions", "Client margins may be used to meet the member's proprietary losses", "Margin needs to be collected only if the client makes a loss", "Margin can be waived for clients who trade frequently"], 0, "Regulations require upfront margin collection from clients and segregation of client funds. Using one client's money for the member's own trades or other clients' dues is not allowed."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "For tax audit purposes, turnover from derivatives trading is generally computed as:", ["The total notional value of all contracts traded", "Only the net profit for the year", "The total initial margin deposited during the year", "The aggregate of favourable and unfavourable differences on settled trades"], 3, "As per the ICAI guidance on tax audit, turnover in derivatives is the absolute total of favourable and unfavourable differences on settled trades. Using notional contract value would hugely overstate it."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "In the currency derivatives segment, the extreme loss margin is collected mainly to cover:", ["The brokerage payable by the client to the trading member", "Losses in unusual market conditions that go beyond what initial margin is designed to cover", "The premium paid by option buyers", "Delays in issuing contract notes to clients"], 1, "Initial margin covers a large one-day loss at a high confidence level. Extreme loss margin is an extra buffer for rarer, larger moves outside that range. It has nothing to do with brokerage or option premium."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "A trader sells 4 USD/INR futures contracts (lot USD 1,000) at 84.00. Settlement prices are 84.15 on day 1 and 83.90 on day 2. His MTM for day 2 alone is:", ["Loss of Rs 600", "Profit of Rs 400", "Profit of Rs 1,000", "Loss of Rs 1,000"], 2, "Day 2 MTM is measured from day 1 settlement price: (84.15 - 83.90) x 1,000 x 4 = Rs 1,000 profit for the short. Day 1 had a loss of Rs 600, which was already settled."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Futures", "As a USD/INR futures contract approaches its expiry, its price relative to the spot rate typically:", ["Moves further away from the spot rate", "Becomes fixed at the price on the first trading day", "Converges towards the spot rate", "Doubles the spot premium seen at launch"], 2, "The futures-spot gap reflects the interest cost of carry for the remaining period. As time to expiry shrinks, carry approaches zero, so the futures price converges to spot and the basis narrows."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Options", "Premium for a USD/INR option traded on an Indian exchange is quoted in:", ["US dollars per rupee", "Percentage of the strike price", "Rupees per lot of USD 100,000", "Rupees per US dollar"], 3, "Like the futures price, the option premium is quoted in rupees per unit of the base currency, i.e. per USD. Total premium payable is the quoted premium multiplied by the lot size of USD 1,000."], ["Trading Mechanism", "A client places a market order to buy 5 USD/INR futures contracts. This order will be executed:", ["Only at the previous day's settlement price", "At the best price available in the order book at that time", "Only if the price falls below the last traded price within the next few minutes of entry", "At the FBIL reference rate for that day"], 1, "A market order has no price limit and matches immediately against the best available counter orders. It guarantees execution if liquidity exists, but not the price, unlike a limit order."], ["Foreign Exchange Derivatives", "A trader buys USD/INR futures on one exchange and simultaneously sells the same contract on another exchange where it is priced higher, locking in a risk-free gain. This trader is acting as:", ["An arbitrageur", "A hedger", "A speculator", "A market regulator"], 0, "An arbitrageur exploits a price mismatch between markets by taking offsetting positions, so there is little net exposure. A speculator takes an open directional view, while a hedger offsets an existing business exposure."], ["Trading Mechanism", "Within what time must a trading member issue a contract note to a client after executing a trade on the client's behalf?", ["Within 7 working days of the trade", "At the end of each calendar month", "Within 24 hours of the trade", "Only when the client asks for it"], 2, "Trading members must issue a contract note, physical or electronic, within 24 hours of execution. It records the trade price, quantity, time and charges and is the client's legal evidence of the trade."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "In the books of a trader, the initial margin paid in cash to the broker for currency futures is generally shown as:", ["An expense in the profit and loss account", "A current asset, such as margin deposit receivable", "Share capital of the business", "A long-term liability"], 1, "Initial margin is a deposit that will be returned when positions are closed, so it is an asset, not an expense. Only realised MTM losses and charges are taken to the profit and loss account."], ["Introduction to Currency Markets", "A quote that shows how many units of foreign currency can be bought with one unit (or 100 units) of the home currency is called:", ["A direct quote", "A cross rate", "A forward outright quote", "An indirect quote"], 3, "An indirect quote expresses the home currency as the base, for example USD 1.19 per INR 100 for an Indian resident. A direct quote shows home currency per unit of foreign currency, like USD/INR = 84.00."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Options", "USD/INR spot is 84.10. What is the intrinsic value of a USD/INR put option with a strike of 83.75?", ["Rs 0.35", "Zero", "Rs 83.75", "Rs 0.35 plus the premium"], 1, "A put has intrinsic value only when the strike is above spot. Here the strike 83.75 is below spot 84.10, so the put is out of the money and its intrinsic value is zero; its whole premium is time value."], ["Trading Mechanism", "A day order for EUR/INR futures that is not executed during the session will:", ["Be carried forward automatically to the next trading day at the same limit price", "Be converted into a market order at the close", "Remain valid until the contract expires", "Be cancelled automatically at the end of the trading day"], 3, "A day order is valid only for the trading day on which it is placed. Any unexecuted quantity is removed from the book at the close, and the client must re-enter it the next day if still required."], ["Regulatory Framework", "FEMA, 1999, which governs foreign exchange transactions in India, replaced which earlier law?", ["Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956", "Foreign Exchange Regulation Act, 1973", "Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934", "Sale of Goods Act, 1930"], 1, "FEMA replaced FERA, 1973, which treated violations as criminal offences. FEMA shifted to a facilitation approach with civil penalties. The RBI Act, 1934 continues in force and was not replaced."], ["Codes of Conduct & Investor Protection", "An investor has a complaint about trades done by his broker in currency derivatives. What should normally be his first step?", ["File a civil suit in the Supreme Court", "Complain directly to the Reserve Bank of India", "Write to the broker's grievance redressal desk", "Stop paying margin on all open positions"], 2, "Investors should first approach the intermediary itself. If not resolved, they can escalate to SEBI's SCORES and then to online dispute resolution. Courts and RBI are not the first point of contact."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Which law defines derivatives as securities and governs recognised stock exchanges on which currency derivatives trade?", ["Foreign Exchange Management Act, 1999", "Banking Regulation Act, 1949", "Companies Act, 2013", "Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956"], 3, "The SCRA, 1956 includes derivatives in the definition of securities and provides for recognition and regulation of stock exchanges. FEMA governs foreign exchange transactions, while the Banking Regulation Act covers banks."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "On currency derivatives trades, GST is charged on:", ["Brokerage and exchange transaction charges", "The full notional value of each contract", "The premium amount received by option writers", "The mark-to-market profit of the client"], 0, "GST applies to services, so it is charged on brokerage, exchange transaction charges and similar fees. It is not levied on the contract value, the premium or the client's trading profit."], ["Codes of Conduct & Investor Protection", "If an investor is not satisfied with the resolution of a complaint against a stock broker, SEBI's framework allows him to seek conciliation and arbitration through:", ["The Online Dispute Resolution (ODR) platform", "The RBI Integrated Ombudsman Scheme", "The Insurance Ombudsman", "The Lok Sabha Petitions Committee"], 0, "SEBI's ODR mechanism connects investors and intermediaries with independent conciliators and arbitrators online. The RBI ombudsman handles banking complaints, and the insurance ombudsman handles insurance cases."], ["Trading Mechanism", "A client wants to buy a large number of contracts but show only a small part of the quantity in the market at a time. He should use:", ["An immediate-or-cancel order", "A stop-loss order", "A disclosed quantity order", "A market-on-close order"], 2, "A disclosed quantity order reveals only part of the total order to the market; as that part is filled, the next part is shown. This reduces market impact. IOC and stop-loss orders do not hide quantity."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "Calendar spread positions in currency futures attract lower margins mainly because:", ["They can be held only intraday and must be squared off before the close of that day's trading session", "The exchange does not guarantee their settlement", "The two legs are in the same currency pair, so their price moves largely offset", "They are settled by physical delivery of currency"], 2, "In a calendar spread, a long and short position in different expiries of the same pair move closely together, so net risk is smaller than two outright positions. The clearing corporation still guarantees them."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Futures", "An Indian importer must pay JPY 5,000,000 in two months and fears the rupee will weaken against the yen. To hedge with JPY/INR futures, he should:", ["Sell 50 contracts", "Buy 50 contracts", "Buy 5,000 contracts", "Sell 5 contracts"], 1, "The importer loses if JPY/INR rises, so he must buy futures. With a lot size of JPY 100,000, he needs 5,000,000 / 100,000 = 50 contracts. Selling would add to his risk, not reduce it."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "A trader has a non-speculative business loss from currency futures that he cannot set off this year. For how many assessment years can it be carried forward?", ["4 assessment years", "2 assessment years", "It cannot be carried forward", "8 assessment years"], 3, "A non-speculative business loss can be carried forward for up to 8 assessment years and set off against business income. Speculative losses can be carried forward for only 4 years."], ["Introduction to Currency Markets", "USD/INR moves from 83.00 to 84.50 over a month. Which statement correctly describes this move?", ["The rupee has appreciated against the US dollar", "The US dollar has appreciated and the rupee has depreciated", "Both currencies have appreciated by the same amount", "The rupee has depreciated only if Indian inflation has also risen in that month"], 1, "A higher USD/INR rate means more rupees are needed to buy one dollar. So the dollar has strengthened (appreciated) and the rupee has weakened (depreciated). Inflation is not needed to describe the move."], ["Strategies Using Currency Derivatives", "An exporter buys a USD/INR 83.50 put at Rs 0.30 and sells an 85.00 call at Rs 0.30, same expiry. Ignoring costs, the effective rate he gets for his dollars at expiry will lie:", ["Between 83.50 and 85.00", "At exactly 84.25 whatever happens", "Anywhere below 83.50 with no floor", "Above 85.00 with no cap"], 0, "This is a zero-cost collar since premiums offset. The put sets a floor at 83.50 and the short call caps gains at 85.00, so the realised rate stays within that band."], ["Codes of Conduct & Investor Protection", "The document that sets out the rights and obligations of the stock broker and the client, as prescribed by SEBI and the exchanges, is:", ["The Memorandum of Association", "The Rights and Obligations document", "The Offer Document", "The Contract Note"], 1, "The Rights and Obligations document, given at account opening, lays out mandatory terms between broker and client. A contract note records individual trades, and an offer document relates to public issues."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "A listed company following Ind AS that holds currency futures not designated in a hedge relationship will generally measure them at:", ["Historical cost until the contract expires", "The lower of cost or market value", "Notional value, disclosed only in the notes to accounts without any entry in the balance sheet", "Fair value, with changes recognised in profit or loss"], 3, "Under Ind AS 109, derivatives are measured at fair value. Unless hedge accounting applies, gains and losses go through profit or loss. Cost-based measurement would hide the derivative's actual value."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Which authority regulates the over-the-counter foreign exchange market and authorised dealer banks in India?", ["The Securities and Exchange Board of India", "The Reserve Bank of India", "The Forward Markets Commission", "The National Stock Exchange"], 1, "RBI regulates OTC forex dealing and authorised dealers under FEMA. SEBI's role is limited to exchange-traded products and market intermediaries. The Forward Markets Commission was merged with SEBI in 2015."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Futures", "Which of the following is a cross-currency pair, not involving the rupee, on which futures are available on Indian exchanges?", ["EUR/USD", "SGD/INR", "AUD/NZD", "CHF/JPY"], 0, "Indian exchanges offer futures on EUR/USD, GBP/USD and USD/JPY as cross-currency pairs. AUD/NZD and CHF/JPY are not listed, and SGD/INR involves the rupee and is not a listed pair either."], ["Introduction to Currency Markets", "Since the unification of exchange rates in 1993, India's exchange rate regime is best described as:", ["A fixed peg to the US dollar revised once a year by the government", "A basket peg with a fixed band announced by the Finance Ministry", "A completely free float in which RBI never buys or sells foreign currency", "A market-determined rate with RBI intervention to curb excess volatility"], 3, "After the LERMS experiment, rates were unified in March 1993 and the rupee became market-determined. RBI intervenes to smooth undue volatility but does not target a fixed level, so it is a managed float."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Futures", "What is the contract (lot) size of a GBP/INR futures contract on Indian exchanges?", ["GBP 1,000", "GBP 10,000", "GBP 100", "GBP 100,000"], 0, "GBP/INR futures, like USD/INR and EUR/INR, have a lot size of 1,000 units of the base currency. Only JPY/INR uses a much larger lot of JPY 100,000 because of the yen's low unit value."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "On which day are the funds for a trading day's MTM obligations in currency futures paid in and paid out?", ["Only on the expiry date of the contract, after the final settlement price is known", "On the next working day (T+1)", "Once a week, on Friday", "Within 30 days of the trade"], 1, "MTM obligations arising on a trading day are paid in and paid out on the following working day through the clearing members' accounts with clearing banks. Waiting until expiry would allow losses to build up."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Futures", "A client holds 5 long GBP/INR futures (lot GBP 1,000). The previous day's settlement price was 106.40 and the final settlement price on expiry is 106.10. The final day's settlement amount is:", ["Rs 1,500 receivable by the client", "Rs 1,500 payable by the client", "Rs 300 payable by the client", "Rs 4,500 payable by the client"], 1, "Earlier days' gains and losses were already settled through daily MTM. On expiry only the move from the last settlement price counts: (106.10 - 106.40) x 1,000 x 5 = -Rs 1,500, a payment by the long holder."], ["Introduction to Currency Markets", "GBP/USD is quoted at 1.2700 / 1.2702 and USD/INR at 84.00 / 84.02. The derived two-way GBP/INR quote is approximately:", ["106.66 / 106.74", "106.70 / 106.70", "106.68 / 106.72", "106.72 / 106.68"], 2, "To buy GBP with INR you buy GBP at 1.2702 and buy USD at 84.02: 1.2702 x 84.02 = 106.72 (ask). The bid uses both bids: 1.2700 x 84.00 = 106.68. Bid must always be below ask."], ["Strategies Using Currency Derivatives", "A trader buys a USD/INR 84.50 put at Rs 0.60 and sells an 84.00 put at Rs 0.30 (lot USD 1,000). What is his maximum loss on one lot?", ["Rs 200", "Rs 300", "Rs 500", "Rs 900"], 1, "This bear put spread costs a net 0.60 - 0.30 = Rs 0.30 per USD, which is lost if USD/INR ends above 84.50. Maximum loss = 0.30 x 1,000 = Rs 300; maximum profit would be (0.50 - 0.30) x 1,000 = Rs 200."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Options", "What is the maximum profit that the writer (seller) of a USD/INR put option can earn?", ["The strike price minus the premium", "The premium received", "Unlimited, if the rupee appreciates sharply", "The strike price multiplied by the lot size"], 1, "A writer's best case is that the option expires worthless, so he keeps the premium received. His potential loss is large if the rate falls well below the strike, but his profit is capped at the premium."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Futures", "For currency futures on Indian exchanges, the daily settlement price is generally based on:", ["The first traded price of the day", "The weighted average price of trades in the last half hour of trading", "The FBIL reference rate published every day", "The highest price traded during the day"], 1, "Daily MTM uses the volume-weighted average price of the last 30 minutes, or a theoretical price if there are no trades. The FBIL reference rate is used for final settlement on expiry, not for daily settlement."], ["Strategies Using Currency Derivatives", "A position of long USD/INR futures combined with a long USD/INR put option behaves most like:", ["A long call option", "A short call option", "A long put option", "A short futures position"], 0, "Long futures gains as the rate rises; the put limits losses if it falls. The combined payoff has limited downside and open upside, the same shape as a long call. This is the protective put strategy."], ["Foreign Exchange Derivatives", "An exporter switches his hedge from a bank forward to exchange-traded USD/INR futures. Which new requirement must he now plan for?", ["Physical delivery of dollars to the exchange at expiry", "Paying an option premium at the start of the hedge", "Obtaining a fresh approval from RBI each time he places an order or modifies an existing one", "Daily cash flows from mark-to-market settlement until the position is closed"], 3, "A forward is settled only at maturity, but futures are marked to market daily, so losses must be paid in cash each day. Currency futures in India are cash-settled in rupees, and no premium is involved."], ["Codes of Conduct & Investor Protection", "Client KYC records collected by one SEBI intermediary can be accessed by other intermediaries through:", ["The Credit Information Bureau", "A KYC Registration Agency (KRA)", "The Registrar of Companies", "The Income-tax e-filing portal"], 1, "KRAs registered with SEBI hold the KYC records of investors, so once KYC is done with one intermediary others can fetch it. This avoids repeated documentation by the client."], ["Codes of Conduct & Investor Protection", "Which action by a trading member's staff would breach the code of conduct?", ["Explaining the risks of currency derivatives to a client in detail before the account is activated for trading", "Sending a contract note within 24 hours of a trade", "Promising a client assured monthly returns from currency futures trading", "Recording client orders before placing them"], 2, "Brokers must not guarantee returns or make misleading statements. Explaining risks, issuing timely contract notes and keeping order records are all expected practices under the regulations."], ["Introduction to Currency Markets", "A foreign exchange deal with a 'tom' value date is settled on:", ["The next business day after the trade date", "The same day as the trade date", "The second business day after the trade date", "Any date both parties agree, beyond one month"], 0, "'Tom' is short for tomorrow: settlement happens on the next business day. 'Cash' (or value today) settles the same day, and spot settles on the second business day after the trade."], ["Exchange Traded Currency Options", "Which option Greek measures the fall in an option's premium as time passes, other things remaining equal?", ["Delta", "Gamma", "Theta", "Rho"], 2, "Theta measures time decay. Delta measures sensitivity to the underlying rate, gamma measures how fast delta changes, and rho measures sensitivity to interest rates."], ["Strategies Using Currency Derivatives", "Writing an 84.00 USD/INR call while holding a long 85.00 call, both expiring together, creates which spread?", ["Bull call spread", "Bear call spread", "Long strangle", "Short straddle"], 1, "Selling the lower-strike call and buying the higher-strike call earns a net credit and profits if USD/INR stays below the lower strike. That is a bear call spread; a bull call spread is the reverse."], ["Trading Mechanism", "Two buy orders for USD/INR futures are in the book at the same price of 84.10. Which will be matched first against an incoming sell order at 84.10?", ["The order with the larger quantity", "The order placed by a clearing member", "The order that was entered earlier", "The orders are matched in proportion to their size"], 2, "Exchanges match orders on price-time priority. The best price gets priority, and at the same price the order that reached the system first is executed first. Order size or member type does not change priority."]]};
